{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"A FIFTH escape shape — and it REFINES this epic's framing, so recording it here rather than as a separate finding.\n\nThis epic currently draws the line as: the gates caught things when \"the ground truth lived in the repo, where the reviewer's read/grep tools could REACH it\", and missed things whose measurement lived in the world. A case found this run shows that line is drawn in the wrong place.\n\nTHE CASE (full detail on eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a): S8's plan asserts \"The model stays Claude; only the route changes\", with a Scope of one env var (the bot service's REBAR_LLM_MODEL). That assertion is FALSE, and provably so from the repo alone: resolve_model routes step > workflow > config; code-review.yaml declares no per-step model so all four passes take cfg.model; review_kernel/verify.py:269 only downgrades Pass-2/Pass-4 to sonnet when model == \"claude-opus-4-8\" EXACTLY, so any bedrock: id disables the downgrade; and config.py:333-341 maps bare \"claude\" -> anthropic, so plan-review.yaml:62-65's bare model_ladder keeps calling Anthropic after the cutover. One env var cannot express the four-pass model mapping the cutover needs.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS TO THIS EPIC: the ground truth here is 100% in-repo and fully reachable — no AWS call, no billing, no world. It was still invisible, because it is FOUR HOPS across FOUR files that the plan never cites. The reviewer pins the ticket's own material plus its direct dependencies' material; S8 cites voter.py:69 and infra paths and nothing on the model-resolution path. So the binding constraint is not REACHABILITY, it is CITATION: the gate reads what the plan points at, and a plan's most dangerous claims are exactly the ones it does not point at, because the author did not know they needed checking.\n\nThat is a sharper and more tractable target than \"world-grounded evidence\". A claim like \"only the route changes\" is an assertion about the system's OWN runtime behaviour, and today nothing asks the plan to show its work for it. It reads to a reviewer as SCOPE DISCIPLINE — a virtue — which is why it survives; the plan is rewarded for the narrow Scope that is itself the defect.\n\nCANDIDATE STORY for this epic, distinct from the existing three children: a criterion (advisory-first per ADR-0054, consistent with this epic's design) that fires when a plan asserts unchanged behaviour across a substrate/route/provider swap WITHOUT citing the resolution path that would make it true. Detection sketch: a swap-shaped plan (provider/route/endpoint/model change) whose Scope touches only configuration, and whose citations include no file on the path that consumes that configuration. The remedy it asks for is cheap and mechanical — cite the resolution chain, or state the assertion as an open question.\n\nNOTE ON TIMING, so this is not misread: S8 has NOT been plan-reviewed yet (plan_review_phase: planning, empty keyring). The gate did not miss this — it has not run. This is a PREDICTED escape, argued from what the gate reads rather than from an observed failure, and it should be labelled as such until the review actually runs on S8. If the review DOES surface the routing gap unprompted, that is direct evidence against this candidate story and it should be dropped."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785435569148187001,"uuid":"b1ff3e06-2c6b-4f9a-b95a-3ded5ca17121"}